WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:12.559
It was pi day last month, and call me an idiot
for not releasing this video then, but I’m

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:13.940
just not a math nerd.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.500
I’m an engineer and all this hype for a
mathematical constant goes a little over my

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.500
head.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.869
Internet drama has been born out of people
for and against pi.

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:28.310
A quick search of YouTube will yield 10s,
literally 10s of videos, teaching you how

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.550
to memorize the digits of pi.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.910
The Guinness World Record for reciting pi
with the most digits has changed hands multiple

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:41.719
times with the current champion, Rajveer Meena
of India, managing 70,000 digits in 10 hours.

00:00:41.719 --> 00:00:43.460
Fair play Rajveer, fair play.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.290
You have finally made it.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.370
Now go use that brain for something actually
useful.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:52.730
Jokes aside, this is a genuinely impressive
act of memory.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:57.260
Pi is a fascinating mathematical constant,
that defies any tricks for memorisation.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.380
It just keeps going and going and going.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.519
It’s infinite and it doesn’t repeat itself.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:07.340
You just have to brute force memorise it.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.720
And while these lads are spending days learning
as many digits off as possible, mathematicians

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.270
are working away at calculating the new longest
string of digits.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:20.830
Pi is found fairly simply by dividing a circle’s
circumference by its diameter.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:27.479
So the diameter of a circle can fit into its
circumference 3.14159 26535 89793 23846 26433

00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:29.590
83279 50288 4197…..

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:36.299
Okay, so you get the point, that is simple
on paper, but in practice it is far more difficult.

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:40.200
Measuring a circle's circumference accurately
is practically impossible.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.540
Even your best attempts with a measuring tape
will be off by the thickness of the measuring

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:44.820
tape.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.600
Archimedes was the first to calculate pi with
any level of accuracy with an ingenious method

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.500
called the “method of exhaustion”, and
yes it was nearly as exhausting as this video’s

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.119
attempts at snarky humour.

00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:58.409
He started estimating pi with squares, which
sounds unconventional, but it makes a lot

00:01:58.409 --> 00:01:59.409
of sense.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:04.190
In the mind of archimedes a circle was simply
a polygon with an infinite number of sides,

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:08.539
so by starting with a polygon with fewer sides
we can get a very rough estimate of pi by

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:11.780
calculating it’s ratio of circumference
to diameter.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.130
Let’s start by placing a square inside of
a circle with its corners touching the circles

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.130
sides.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.250
We can then find the ratio of this low estimate
of pi by dividing the sum of the squares sides

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:27.250
by its diagonal diameter, which in this case
is (4/root 2): 2.828 This is our lowest estimate

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.400
of pi.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.570
Now let’s place a square with it’s side
touching the circles side, and this time we

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:36.500
will divide the sum of the squares sides by
the length of one of the squares sides, which

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.410
gives us 4, the number of sides a square has.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:44.290
In boths of these cases we are dividing by
the circles diameter, that figure is accurate.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:47.900
What we are lacking in accuracy is the measurement
of the circumference.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.560
The inside squares perimeter is too small,
and the outside squares perimeter is too big,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.960
but now we know pi lies between these two
numbers.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.290
Now we just need to narrow it down, and we
can do that by increasing the number of sides

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:01.710
of the polygon.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.480
Each time we add a side those two figures
will get more accurate, and eventually the

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.620
two numbers will start overlapping in their
digits.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.920
This is how we got our first known digits
of pi.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.030
This continued on for a couple of centuries,
with mathematicians out exhausting each other

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:20.670
until eventually someone started using computers
and now we are just exhausting them.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:25.650
We now have 2.7 trillion digits of pi calculated[2],
and for some reason through these millenia,

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.420
an engineer never stood up and yelled:

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.420
“shtop.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:29.690
For the love of all that is holy stop.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:30.760
We have enough digits.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.850
We don’t need any more.

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:36.090
We don’t have any circles big enough to
justify this level of accuracy, go to bed

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.490
ye lunatics.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.240
~deep breath~"

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.420
Because in the end of the day, that’s what
pi is used for.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.050
It’s for calculating the circumference and
area of circles.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.920
It’s for converting degrees to radians,
and this is where the arts of mathematics

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:49.930
and engineering differ.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:54.330
While mathematicians obsess over accuracy
to the trillionth digit, engineers aim for

00:03:54.330 --> 00:04:02.680
“good enough”, and good enough turns out
to be 3.141592653589793 for the people that

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.720
work with the biggest circles.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:05.720
NASA.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.780
Let’s take the distance to Voyager 1, which
is currently about 21.7 billion (21690753480.975746)

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.950
kilometres away in interstellar space, as
our radius.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:18.250
Say we want to calculate the circumference
of a circle with a radius this large.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.150
What difference would adding one extra digit
of pi provide?

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:26.340
That’s the difference between 15 decimal
places and 16 decimal places.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.600
This is actually tough enough to calculate
with a calculator or excel as both are limited

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.540
in the number of decimal places they can calculate.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.120
So using this online high precision calculator
we can find that the circumference of this

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:46.270
circle will be about 136 billion kilometres,
and if we use on extra digit it will be 8.67

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.790
millimeters closer to the actual value.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:51.990
That’s tiny and we just travelled out of
the solar system.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.620
That’s why JPL and NASA don’t need any
more figures and the chances are you don’t

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.060
need anymore that 3.1416.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.020
So I’m gonna be the engineer that yells
shtap.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.020
Please stop.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.250
We have better things to be worrying about,
pi is literally infinite and we are never

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.340
going to reach the end.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.310
What is the point.

